(window.LADDER_CARDS["41·now"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["41·now"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"41·now.1.1","q":"What does the beaver-outruns technique say? Why would a machine computing a bound on BB decide the blank-tape question?","a":"No machine computes a function f with f(n) ≥ BB(n) for every n; for X with n states on a blank tape, compute f(n) and run X for f(n) steps: if X stops it stops within BB(n) ≤ f(n) steps, so not stopped by then means never, and the blank-tape question would be decided, against 41e1","w":["Some machine computes BB itself; the bound decides nothing, since BB is small","No machine computes any function above n²; the bound would decide the blank-tape question by counting states","No machine computes f with f(n) ≥ BB(n); the bound would decide the question because every machine stops within f(n) steps"],"why":"BB has a value at every n and passes every function a machine computes."},
 {"u":"41·now.2.1","q":"What two things had to be proved to settle BB(5)? What does the closed-patterns technique say, and which technique of 4l is it?","a":"That one five-state machine stops after exactly 47 176 870 steps, found in 1989, and that every other five-state machine either stops sooner or never stops; if a set of configurations holds the start, holds the next of each of its members, and holds no configuration whose state is a stop state, then the machine never stops; it is 4l’s kept-statement technique","w":["That every five-state machine stops, and that one takes the longest; the technique says a pattern seen twice repeats; it is 4l’s falling measure","That some machine runs for ever, and that none stops later than 47 million steps; the technique says a closed set must hold a stop state; it is 4l’s recipe","That BB(4) = 107 and BB(5) is larger; the technique says every run leaves its set in the end; it is 4l’s five parts"],"why":"In 2024 the whole sorting was checked by a proof checker with a small core."},
 {"u":"41·now.3.1","q":"What does the axioms-outrun technique say? Why would reaching BB(n) = k let T reach that M_T never stops?","a":"M_T never stops; T does not reach that M_T never stops; and if M_T has n states, T reaches no true statement of the form BB(n) = k; from BB(n) = k, T could run M_T for k steps and reach that it has not stopped, a true bounded statement, and so that it never stops, which is Con_T, against the no-self-certificate technique (38j)","w":["M_T stops after BB(n) steps; T reaches that it stops; BB(n) = k would let T find the proof of 0 = 1","M_T never stops, and T reaches that it never stops; BB(n) = k adds nothing","M_T may or may not stop; T reaches BB(n) = k for every n; that M_T never stops is then false"],"why":"BB(n) is still one number; T cannot find it."},
 {"u":"41·now.4.1","q":"What is the halving and tripling rule, and what is known about it? Why can BB(6) not be settled without settling a question of that kind?","a":"If the number is even, halve it; if odd, triple it and add 1; every start below about 2.95 × 10²⁰ has been checked to reach 1, and nobody can prove it for every start; in 2024 a six-state machine was found that stops exactly when a run of a rule much like this one ever has more odd values than twice its even ones, which is open","w":["Halve every number; it is proved that every start reaches 1; BB(6) is settled at 47 million","If even, halve; if odd, triple and add 1; it is proved false, since 27 never reaches 1; BB(6) depends on 27","If even, double; if odd, add 1; nothing is known; BB(6) was settled in 2025 as 2↑↑↑5"],"why":"From 27 the run takes 111 steps and climbs to 9232."},
 {"u":"41·now.5.1","q":"Why does “no machine computes BB” not mean that BB(5) cannot be known? Why must a set of patterns hold the next of each of its members?","a":"Each value is one number; no single machine gives every value, and BB(5) was proved; and without the next of each member the run can leave the set, so the set proves nothing about the run","w":["BB(5) cannot be known, only guessed; and the set need only hold the start","A machine computes BB up to 5 and no further; and the set must hold the next so that it is finite","BB(5) is known because 5 is odd; and the set holds the next of each member so that a stop state is in it"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["41·now2"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["41·now2"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"41·now2.1.1","q":"What does the one-test technique say? Why would a fast method for three-colouring settle the question?","a":"P equals NP exactly when the satisfiable statements are fast: every set in NP fast-reduces to them by the tableau technique, and a fast reduction then a fast decider is fast by the power-stays technique; three-colouring is NP-complete too, by 41k, so a proof that it is fast, or that it is not, settles all of them","w":["P equals NP exactly when some set in NP is fast; three-colouring is fast already, so the question is settled","P equals NP exactly when satisfiability is not fast; three-colouring settles nothing, since it is about maps","P equals NP when every set is decided; three-colouring would settle it because maps are the hardest inputs"],"why":"The whole question is one question about one set."},
 {"u":"41·now2.2.1","q":"What does the clause-branch technique say, and where does the recurrence T(n) ≤ T(n − 1) + T(n − 2) + T(n − 3) come from? Why is 1.307ⁿ fast for 100 letters and hopeless for 1000?","a":"A statement in clauses of three with n letters is decided in about 1.84ⁿ steps; pick a clause a ∨ b ∨ c not yet true and branch: a true; or a false and b true; or a, b false and c true; the branches fix 1, 2 and 3 letters; 1.307¹⁰⁰ ≈ 4.2 · 10¹¹, but 1.307¹⁰⁰⁰ ≈ 1.9 · 10¹¹⁶, against 1000³ = 10⁹ for a power of n","w":["A statement in clauses of three is decided in n³ steps; the recurrence comes from the three letters of a clause being tried at once; 1.307ⁿ is a power of n","A statement with n letters is decided in 2ⁿ steps only; the recurrence comes from halving the letters; 1.307ⁿ is slow for 100 letters and fast for 1000","A statement in clauses of three is decided in about 1.84ⁿ steps; the recurrence comes from three clauses being dropped at each branch; 1.307ⁿ is hopeless at 1000 because the machine runs out of tape"],"why":"x³ = x² + x + 1 has the one real root x ≈ 1.839."},
 {"u":"41·now2.3.1","q":"What does the prefix-undo technique say, and why is B in NP? Why would P equal to NP make short proofs as easy to find as to check?","a":"If P equals NP, then for every fast function f there is a fast machine that, given y = f(x) with x of known length, finds some x′ with f(x′) = y; B is the set of (y, p) such that some x beginning with p has f(x) = y, with x as the certificate, checked fast; “PA reaches φ by a proof of at most n marks” is in NP, with the proof as certificate, so the same letter-by-letter questions would find the proof","w":["If P equals NP, every function is fast; B is in NP because it is decided; proofs would be found by trying every string","If P equals NP, f can be undone only when it is one-to-one; B is in NP because p is short; proofs would still take as long as now","If P differs from NP, every fast f can be undone fast; B is not in NP; short proofs are easy to find already"],"why":"n fast questions find x′, one letter at a time."},
 {"u":"41·now2.4.1","q":"What is the evidence for the bet that P differs from NP, and what kind of induction is it? Why can a diagonal argument like 41h4’s not settle the question alone?","a":"Thousands of NP-complete questions from every field, studied by people who wanted fast methods, and none found: evidence of the kind 30w weighs, not a proof; an argument that runs machines and does the opposite never looks inside them, so it works as well for machines with any free question, and in 1975 a set A was shown for which P and NP with free questions about A are equal, and a set B for which they differ","w":["A proof from 1975; it is induction on n; the diagonal settles the question for machines with free questions","A poll of researchers, which is a proof by vote; the diagonal fails because NP has no machines","No evidence at all; it is shape-induction; the diagonal settles it once the padding is long enough"],"why":"A proof that settles the question must fail for one of A or B."},
 {"u":"41·now2.5.1","q":"Why do fast programs on real statements not show P equals NP? Why would P equal to NP not pick every lock if the power were large?","a":"They are fast on statements with structure, and the question is about every statement; and n¹⁰⁰ is fast by the definition and useless by any clock, so locks are picked only if the power is small","w":["They show it for millions of letters, which is enough; and any power of n picks every lock at once","Real statements are the worst cases; and a large power is faster than a small one","The programs use coins; and locks do not depend on P and NP at all"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["41·now3"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["41·now3"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"41·now3.1.1","q":"What does the move-search technique say, and which line uses the claim held in trust from 34m1? Why does the search not decide whether a drawing is untied?","a":"The drawings of the unknot are listed by a machine: from the ring O, for k = 1, 2, 3, … make every string of k moves and print every drawing reached; the line “D is the unknot, so some string of moves joins O to D” uses 34m1; if D is knotted it is never printed, and no moment comes when waiting longer is pointless, unless there is a bound on the length of the string","w":["The knotted drawings are listed by a machine; the first line uses 34m1; the search decides, since each k has finitely many strings","The drawings of the unknot are decided by counting crossings; no line uses 34m1; the search fails because drawings are endless","The drawings of the unknot are listed; the line that each drawing has finitely many places for a move uses 34m1; the search decides once k passes the number of crossings"],"why":"Listing is not deciding; some drawings of the ring must first get more crossings."},
 {"u":"41·now3.2.1","q":"What does the bounded-untying technique say, and why does each drawing along the way have at most n + 2M crossings? Why does the string of moves count as a certificate, though for n = 10 it could be 10³⁷ moves long?","a":"Whether a drawing is the unknot is decided, and it is in NP, given the bound of at most M = (236n)¹¹ moves, held in trust from 2015; a twist adds at most one crossing, a poke two and a slide none, so M moves add at most 2M; the string has at most M moves, each drawing a power of n long, checked move by move in a power of n steps: short in the sense of 41h, though no one would write it","w":["Untied is listed and not decided; each move adds n crossings; the string is no certificate, since it is too long","Untied is decided and fast; no move adds a crossing; the string is a certificate because it has at most n moves","Untied is in NP only for n below 10; a slide adds two crossings; the string is a certificate because the checker has unlimited time"],"why":"Decided in principle, never by this search in practice."},
 {"u":"41·now3.3.1","q":"What is a p-colouring, and what does the many-colour technique say? How do five colours show the figure-eight knot is knotted, when three colours could not?","a":"A labelling of each strand by 0, 1, …, p − 1 so that at every crossing twice the over label less the two under labels is a multiple of p; the count cₚ(D) is the same for every drawing of a knot, and is p for the unknot, so a p-colouring that is not all alike is a certificate that a drawing is knotted; the figure-eight has c₃ = 3, only the all-alike ones, but c₅ = 25 ≠ 5","w":["A colouring of the crossings with p colours; cₚ changes with the drawing; the figure-eight has c₅ = 5, so it is the ring","A labelling of strands so that the three labels at a crossing are equal; cₚ is p for every knot; five colours catch the figure-eight because it has five crossings","A labelling of strands by 0 to p − 1 with twice the over label less the under labels a multiple of p; cₚ is 0 for the unknot; the figure-eight has c₃ = 9 and c₅ = 5"],"why":"The overhand knot has c₃ = 9: three colours catch it, five and seven do not."},
 {"u":"41·now3.4.1","q":"What is known, and what is open, about how fast untied can be decided? Why do knot tables need counts like cₚ and X, and not only the move search?","a":"Untied is decided, has a certificate both ways and is settled fast in practice; whether it is fast, with a fixed power of n as its bound on every drawing, is open, and a method announced in 2021 has a bound of n raised to a power that grows like the number of digits of n; a table is checked by computing such counts until every pair is told apart, since the move search only lists sameness and never says that two drawings differ","w":["Untied is proved fast; nothing is open; tables use counts because the move search is wrong","Untied is not decided; whether it is listed is open; tables use the move search alone","Untied is decided within n steps; whether it is in NP is open; tables need counts because drawings cannot be listed"],"why":"There are 249 knots with 3 to 10 crossings that are not two knots joined end to end."},
 {"u":"41·now3.5.1","q":"Why does cₚ(D) = p for every p not show that D is the ring? Why is “decided” not the same as “fast” for untied?","a":"A knot with ten crossings has cₚ = p for every p, so every colour count calls it the ring; and decided says the machine stops, while fast says how soon: for ten crossings the bound here allows 10³⁷ moves","w":["cₚ = p for every p does show D is the ring; and decided sets are always fast","Only c₃ matters; and untied is fast because it is in NP","No knot has cₚ = p; and decided means fast for drawings with few crossings"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
/* end phase F und_r41 */
