(window.LADDER_CARDS["42·now"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42·now"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42·now.1.1","q":"Why does one swap free a colour when the dot has four neighbours? Why can two swaps done one after the other fail when each alone would work?","a":"With at most four neighbours one two-colour swap works as in 42d: either the swap frees a colour, or a chain closes a cycle that cuts off the other pair, whose swap then frees one; with five neighbours coloured 1, 2, 3, 4, 2 the proof made two swaps, and after the first, which recolours dots, the chains the second relied on can change, so both together can join what was apart","w":["Four neighbours always use three colours; two swaps fail because they undo each other exactly","One swap works because four colours are free; two swaps fail only on maps with five countries","A dot with four neighbours needs no swap; two swaps never fail, and the 1879 proof stands"],"why":"The flaw was found in 1890, and the proof kept five colours."},
 {"u":"42·now.2.1","q":"Why do the charges 6 − d add to 12? Why does moving charge prove that some piece must appear?","a":"With every face a triangle, 3F = 2E, so the flat-count technique gives E = 3V − 6, and Σ d = 2E = 6V − 12, so Σ (6 − d) = 12; moving charge between dots keeps the total 12, so if a rule for moving can be shown to leave every dot at 0 or below, something the rule assumed absent must be present","w":["Each dot has charge 12/V; moving charge changes the total until a piece appears","The charges add to 12 because a flat graph has 12 dots of five links; moving charge shows every dot has six links","The charges add to 0; moving charge proves nothing about pieces"],"why":"So a flat graph of triangles with every dot of five or more links has a five next to a five or six."},
 {"u":"42·now.3.1","q":"What does it mean for a piece to be reducible? Why does an unavoidable list of reducible pieces prove that four colours suffice?","a":"A piece is reducible if it cannot appear in a smallest flat graph needing five colours: whenever the rest is coloured in four, the colouring can be changed by swaps and finished on the piece; a list is unavoidable if every such smallest graph has one of its pieces; so no smallest graph needing five can exist, and none needing five exists at all","w":["A piece is reducible if it has few dots; the list proves four colours by colouring each piece","A piece is reducible if it appears in every graph; an unavoidable list shows five colours are needed","A piece is reducible if it can be taken out; the list proves it only for graphs on the list"],"why":"One ring of 14 dots has 199 291 colourings to finish, up to renaming the colours."},
 {"u":"42·now.4.1","q":"What did the computer check in 1976, and what was found by hand? Where does the trust rest in the 2005 proof?","a":"The computer checked each of near two thousand pieces reducible, ring colouring by ring colouring, in some 1200 hours, while the unavoidable list was found partly by hand with rules for moving charge; in 2005 the whole proof was written for a proof checker, so the trust rests on the checker’s small core and the written statement","w":["The computer found the list and people checked each piece; the 2005 proof rests on a faster machine","The computer coloured every map; nothing was done by hand; the 2005 proof rests on the 1976 programs","The computer checked 633 pieces in 1976; the 2005 proof needs no trust at all"],"why":"The trust moves from a reader to a check anyone can repeat."},
 {"u":"42·now.5.1","q":"Why did the 1979 argument call the computer proof an experiment? What does the small core of a proof checker change, and what does it not change?","a":"A proof, it said, is something a person can survey, read, follow and be convinced by, and a computer run cannot be surveyed, so accepting it is trusting an experiment; the 2005 check narrows what must be trusted to a small core that people can read and a statement of what was proved; it does not give understanding: a list of 633 pieces says that four colours suffice, and does not say why","w":["Because the computer made errors; the small core removes every doubt and explains the result","Because the proof used measurements of real maps; the core changes nothing","Because no one ran it twice; the core makes the proof short enough to read"],"why":"Whether the knowing is the same kind stays open."},
 {"u":"42·now.6.1","q":"Why is the unavoidable-pair technique false for flat graphs with a dot of four links? Why does a computer check not remove trust?","a":"It is proved only when every dot has five or more links; otherwise the graph may have a dot of four or fewer and no such pair; and the trust moves to the program and the core: it does not vanish","w":["It is true for every flat graph; and a computer check needs no trust","A dot of four links is always next to a five; and trust is removed once two programs agree","Flat graphs have no dots of four links; and a check is only a guess"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["42·now2"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42·now2"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42·now2.1.1","q":"Why can any group of four all linked be moved to one containing 0 and 1, 2, 4 or 8? Why does multiplying by 3 turn a group of four all unlinked into one all linked?","a":"Adding the same number to every dot keeps every link, so one link of the group can be moved to start at 0 and end at a square remainder s, and x ↦ −x keeps links too, so s can be taken among 1, 2, 4, 8; 3 times the eight square remainders gives exactly the eight differences that are not, and 3 times those gives the squares back, so x ↦ 3x shuffles the 17 dots carrying links to non-links and non-links to links","w":["Any four dots can be renamed freely; multiplying by 3 keeps every link","Adding a number changes the links, so the group must be searched for; multiplying by 3 sends every dot to 0","The group can be moved because 17 is odd; multiplying by 3 turns unlinked into linked because 3 is a square remainder"],"why":"So R(4, 4) > 17, and with the upper bound, R(4, 4) = 18."},
 {"u":"42·now2.2.1","q":"Why does counting up to renaming the dots still leave about 10²¹⁹ classes? What would it take to prove R(5, 5) ≥ 44?","a":"K₄₃ has 903 links and so 2⁹⁰³ ≈ 10^271.8 colourings, and each class under renaming holds at most 43! ≈ 10^52.8 of them, by the class-times-keepers technique; a colouring of K₄₃ with no one-colour K₅ would prove R(5, 5) ≥ 44","w":["There are 43! colourings and 2⁹⁰³ renamings; R(5, 5) ≥ 44 needs every colouring of K₄₃ checked","Renaming leaves 903 classes; R(5, 5) ≥ 44 needs a proof that K₄₄ has a one-colour K₅","Each class holds exactly one colouring; R(5, 5) ≥ 44 is already proved"],"why":"Known: 43 ≤ R(5, 5) ≤ 46."},
 {"u":"42·now2.3.1","q":"What are the two bases of 42j’s bounds, and which moved in 2023? Why does a base of 3.8 matter more than any power of k in front of 4^k?","a":"As k-th roots the answer lies between √2 ≈ 1.41, from the average-below-one technique, and 4, from the party-number technique; in 2023 the upper base fell below 4 for the first time, to 4 − 2⁻⁷, and later to 3.8 for large k; exponentials outgrow powers: (3.8/4)¹⁰⁰ ≈ 0.0059, and it keeps shrinking, beating every bound of the form c · 4^k/k^m","w":["The bases are 2 and 3; the lower one moved in 2023; a base matters less than the power in front","The bases are √2 and 4; both moved in 2023; 3.8 matters because it is a whole number of tenths","The bases are 1 and 4; neither has moved; a power of k in front beats any change of base"],"why":"The lower bound has not moved its base since 1947."},
 {"u":"42·now2.4.1","q":"Why does one colouring of K₁₇ prove R(4, 4) > 17? Why does the 2023 bound not settle the size of R(k, k)?","a":"R(4, 4) > 17 says only that some colouring of K₁₇ has no one-colour group of four, and one colouring with none is enough; and the 2023 bound lowers the upper base: the gap from √2 to 3.8 is open","w":["Every colouring of K₁₇ must be checked; and the 2023 bound gives R(k, k) exactly","One colouring proves R(4, 4) = 17; and the 2023 bound moved the lower base to 3.8","A random colouring of K₁₇ has none; and the bound settles it for large k"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
/* end phase F und_r42 */
