(window.LADDER_CARDS["43·now"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["43·now"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"43·now.1.1","q":"Why does the prime-count technique say the average gap near x is about ln x? What does the long-gap technique say, and why is n! + j never prime for 2 ≤ j ≤ n?","a":"If about one number in ln x near x is prime, the primes there are about ln x apart on average; for every n ≥ 2, the n − 1 numbers n! + 2, n! + 3, …, n! + n are none of them prime; j is one of the factors of n!, so j divides n! + j, which is larger than j","w":["Because every gap is exactly ln x; the long-gap technique says gaps are at most n; n! + j is not prime because n! is even","Because π(x) = ln x; the numbers n! + 1 to n! + n are all prime; n! + j is prime only for j = 2","Because the gaps shrink as x grows; long gaps come only after n!; n! + j is not prime because j is"],"why":"With n = 7: 5042 to 5047, six in a row."},
 {"u":"43·now.2.1","q":"Why is n, n + 2, n + 4 prime only for n = 3? What does the covered-hours technique say, and why do only the clocks of primes up to k need checking?","a":"On the clock of 3 the three numbers sit at three different hours, so one is at hour 0 and is divisible by 3: only 3, 5, 7 escape, by being 3 itself; if a pattern fills every hour of the clock of some prime p, then n + h₁, …, n + h_k are all prime for at most finitely many n; k offsets cannot fill more than k hours, so only clocks of primes p ≤ k can be covered","w":["Because one of them is always even; a covered clock gives endlessly many all-prime n; every prime clock must be checked","Because n + 4 is never prime; the technique says a pattern is prime only once; only the clock of 2 needs checking","Because 3, 5, 7 are the only odd primes in a row; the technique says no pattern is all prime twice; clocks above k are covered"],"why":"0, 2, 6 is admissible; 0, 2, 4 is not."},
 {"u":"43·now.3.1","q":"Why does a prime larger than k never sit at hour 0 of the clock of a prime p ≤ k? Why was the first bound on gaps the width of a pattern and not a gap anyone saw?","a":"It is a prime larger than k ≥ p, so p does not divide it; the proof of 2013 showed that an admissible pattern holds two primes endlessly often, and took the 3.5 million primes from 3 500 017 on, whose last is 63 374 611: the width 59 874 594, under seventy million, is the bound, the width of an admissible pattern and not a measured gap","w":["Because primes are odd; the first bound was the largest gap in a table of primes","Because p is larger than the prime; the bound was the gap after 63 374 611","Because hour 0 holds no numbers; the bound was the average gap near seventy million"],"why":"With k = 3: 5, 7, 11, width 6."},
 {"u":"43·now.4.1","q":"What was proved in 2013 and 2014, and which part does this page hold in trust? What does the width-bound technique say, and why does it need the narrowest admissible pattern?","a":"That for k at least 50 every admissible pattern of k offsets has, for endlessly many n, at least two of n + h₁, …, n + h_k prime, and the page holds that result in trust; if so, the width of the narrowest admissible pattern of k offsets is reached or beaten by gaps between primes endlessly often, since both primes lie in the window; the bound is the pattern’s width, so the narrowest known pattern gives the best bound, 246","w":["That twin primes are endless, proved whole on the page; the width-bound technique gives gaps of exactly 246; the narrowest pattern is needed to be admissible","That every pattern holds k primes; nothing is in trust; the technique gives a gap of 2; the narrowest pattern is the only one","That gaps are at most 246 everywhere; the weighing is in trust; the technique bounds the longest gap; the narrowest pattern has the most primes"],"why":"70 million, then 4680, then 600, then 246."},
 {"u":"43·now.5.1","q":"Why does the bound 246 not say which gap recurs? Why can no table of twin primes, however long, prove they never end?","a":"Some gap of at most 246 comes endlessly often, and which one is not known; and counts of a finite table prove nothing about the endless","w":["The gap 246 itself recurs; and a long enough table is a proof","Because 246 is even; and tables of twins have errors","Because every gap up to 246 recurs; and twins are known to end"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["43·now2"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["43·now2"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"43·now2.1.1","q":"Why is li(x), the area under 1/ln t, a natural count of the primes? What does the parts-count technique say about li(x) against x / ln x?","a":"The share of primes near t is about 1/ln t, and adding that share up from 2 to x gives the area; li(x) = x/ln x − 2/ln 2 + the area under 1/(ln t)² from 2 to x, so li(x) exceeds x/ln x by about x/(ln x)²","w":["Because li(x) counts the primes exactly; li(x) = x/ln x for every x","Because 1/ln t is the gap between primes; li(x) is below x/ln x by about ln x","Because the area under 1/t is ln x; li(x) = x/(ln x)²"],"why":"At 10⁶: li is off by 129, x/ln x by 6116."},
 {"u":"43·now2.2.1","q":"Why is the spread squared of one toss q(1 − q)? What does the spread-of-count technique predict for the error of li(x) at a million, and how does the true error compare?","a":"For a toss X that is 0 or 1, X² = X, so E X² = q and the spread squared is q − q²; spreads squared of independent tosses add, so the pretend count strays from li(x) by about √li(x), near 270 at a million; the true error, 129, sits well inside that cloud","w":["Because a toss is fair; it predicts an error of 6116, and the true error is larger","Because q is small; it predicts no error at all, and the true error is 129","Because E X = 1; it predicts an error near 78 627, and the true error is outside the cloud"],"why":"The model is a tool, not a fact about primes."},
 {"u":"43·now2.3.1","q":"Why does the zero ρ = β + iγ give a term of size x^β? What does the hypothesis of 1859 say, and why does it fix the size of the error in counting primes?","a":"x^ρ = e^(β ln x) e^(iγ ln x), and the turning e^(iγ ln x) has size 1, so |x^ρ| = x^β; the hypothesis is that every such zero has β = ½; the error π(x) − li(x) is a sum of one term x^ρ/ρ for each zero, so with every β = ½ each wave has size √x/|ρ|, and the error stays below a fixed multiple of √x ln x, while one zero with β = 0.6 would give a wave growing like x^0.6","w":["Because γ is small; the hypothesis says ζ has no zeros; it fixes the error at 0","Because x^ρ = x^γ; the hypothesis says every zero has β = 1; the error then grows like x","Because the turning has size β; the hypothesis says the zeros are whole numbers; it fixes the error at ln x"],"why":"At 10⁹ the bound is 26 075 and the error is 1700."},
 {"u":"43·now2.4.1","q":"Why does the coin model predict too few twins? What does the twin-correction technique say, and why do the clocks of different primes multiply?","a":"It treats the hours of n and n + 2 as independent, and they are not; on the clock of an odd prime p, n and n + 2 both miss hour 0 for p − 2 of the p hours of n, against (p − 1)²/p if independent, and on the clock of 2 the shares are ½ against ¼, so the clocks raise the twin count by 2 times the product of 1 − 1/(p − 1)² over the odd primes; the clocks of different primes are independent by the remainder technique, so the ratios multiply","w":["Because it forgets that primes are odd only; the correction divides the count by 2; the clocks add","Because it counts each twin twice; the correction is the product of 1 − 1/p; the clocks multiply because primes are endless","Because the coin is not fair; the correction is 1/(ln n)²; the clocks of different primes do not bear on each other, so nothing multiplies"],"why":"1.3203 × 6247 = 8248, against the true 8169."},
 {"u":"43·now2.5.1","q":"Why does checking ten trillion zeros not settle the hypothesis? Why is π(x) < li(x), true for every x anyone has computed, still false?","a":"It is a finite check, and the hypothesis is about endlessly many zeros; and π(x) < li(x) fails somewhere below 10³¹⁷: the sign turns over endlessly often","w":["Ten trillion zeros do settle it; and π(x) < li(x) is true for every x","Because the zeros were checked by machine; and π(x) < li(x) fails at 10⁹","Because only odd zeros count; and π(x) = li(x) from 10¹⁹ on"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
/* end phase F und_r43 */
